use std
into kern.amni
dialect js

This file is a library built for the web as a wasm module; its exported functions serve the Amni-CAD modeling kernel, which runs in JavaScript beside OpenCascade.

Module state: an f64 array `F` of 9 zeros (the sketch plane origin, its unit x axis and its unit normal, each as x, y, z), an f64 array `OUT` of 8 zeros, an f64 array `Q` of 18 zeros, an f64 array `BB` of 6 zeros (a box: minimum x, y, z then maximum x, y, z) and an f64 growable array `VX` that starts empty (mesh vertex coordinates, x, y, z per vertex).

The exported function `kn_views()` registers the arrays JavaScript reads or writes: f64 view 1 `F`, f64 view 2 `OUT`, f64 view 3 `Q`, f64 view 4 `VX` and f64 view 5 `BB`, each through the external function kn_view_f64 (import name "kn_view_f64") that takes an i32 id and the array.

The exported function `kn_twist(mode, dir, depth, turns, pitch, angle, left, cx, cy)` plans an extrude that may twist as it goes. mode, dir and left are i32 and the rest are reals. mode is 0 for no twist, 1 for a number of turns, 2 for a pitch (length per turn) and 3 for a total angle in degrees; dir is 0 when the profile is pushed along the normal, 1 when against it and 2 when both ways; left is 1 for a left-hand twist. The total twist a in degrees is 0.0 for mode 0, turns times 360.0 for mode 1, depth divided by pitch times 360.0 for mode 2 when pitch is greater than 0.0 (0.0 otherwise), and angle for mode 3; a is negated when left is 1. The start shift s along the normal is 0.0 for dir 0, depth for dir 1 and depth divided by 2.0 for dir 2. With o, x and n the three vectors in `F` and y the cross product of n and x, it writes OUT[0] equal to a, OUT[1] equal to minus a times s divided by depth (0.0 when depth is 0.0), OUT[2], OUT[3], OUT[4] equal to the start origin o minus n times s, and OUT[5], OUT[6], OUT[7] equal to that start origin plus cx times x plus cy times y. It returns 1 when a is not 0.0, otherwise 0.

The exported function `kn_square(depth, a, cu, cv, tu, tv)` takes six reals and plans a twisted extrude whose section stays square to its helical path; call it after kn_twist. With x and n the unit x axis and the unit normal in `F`, y the cross product of n and x, and s the start origin held in OUT[2], OUT[3], OUT[4]: the axis point p is s plus tu times x plus tv times y, the profile center c is s plus cu times x plus cv times y, w is c minus p and r is the length of w. When r is below 1e-9, or a is 0.0, or depth is not above 0.0, it returns 0 and writes nothing. Otherwise e is w divided by r, t is the cross product of n and e, th is a converted from degrees to radians, b is the two-argument arctangent of r times th and depth (atan2 with r times th first), and u is t times the cosine of b minus n times the sine of b. The tilted x axis X is e times the dot product of x and e plus u times the dot product of x and t; the tilted y axis Y is built the same way from y; N is the cross product of X and Y. It writes Q[0], Q[1], Q[2] equal to c minus cu times X minus cv times Y, Q[3], Q[4], Q[5] equal to X, Q[6], Q[7], Q[8] equal to N, Q[9] equal to r, Q[10] equal to depth times 360.0 divided by the absolute value of a, Q[11] equal to 1.0 when a is below 0.0 and 0.0 otherwise, Q[12], Q[13], Q[14] equal to p, and Q[15], Q[16], Q[17] equal to e. It returns 1.

The exported function `kn_verts(n)` takes an i32 vertex count n: `VX` is emptied, gets `reserve`(3 times n), and is resized to 3 times n entries equal to 0.0; then it calls `kn_views` and returns 0.

The exported function `kn_tight(n, tol)` takes an i32 vertex count n and a real tolerance tol and tightens the box in `BB` (filled by JavaScript from the modeling kernel, which can overshoot curved swept faces) using the first n vertices in `VX`, where vertex i has coordinates VX[3 times i], VX[3 times i plus 1] and VX[3 times i plus 2]. When n is 0 it returns 0 and changes nothing. Otherwise, for each axis k from 0 to 2, lo is the smallest and hi the largest coordinate k over those vertices; BB[k] becomes lo when BB[k] is below lo minus 2.0 times tol, and BB[3 plus k] becomes hi when BB[3 plus k] is above hi plus 2.0 times tol. It returns 1.

`main()` does nothing. JavaScript drives it through its exported functions.
